joining me now from glasgow is jack  crozier whose five-year-old sister emma  died in the dunblane massacre in 1996.  16 children died there and their teacher  was killed he is now a gun violence  prevention advocate and jack it's really  good to talk to you today thank you very  much for joining us yesterday must have  been a really tough day for you and your  family  it's tough for our family and everyone  from dunblane but it's so much harder  for everyone in america and the families  in texas are having to go through this  not just texas but all the other  communities that have to do this every  single week  is there anything that anyone can do to  help these families anything anyone can  say  that that in any way eases the pain  there's  everyone offers thoughts and prayers and  it's not enough  it's never been enough and it's never  going to be enough and the only thing  that we can do is take action  here in the uk it's hard to support  because we're not directly involved  anymore but there's communities and  there's campaigners out in america that  are doing such amazing work and they're  the ones that  need our support just now  because of course after dunblane the uk  government did bring in legislation to  change gun laws and also in australia  when there was a school shooting there  they brought in laws and both of our  countries mercifully haven't had an  event like that since but it wasn't  necessarily plain sailing to get that  legislation through was it  absolutely not so it wasn't until the  17th of december 1997 before handguns  were banned in the uk  at 645 days after dunblane that's how  long it took to get changed here in the  uk when there was such strong political  will and it takes time but america's had  time they've had so much time it happens  time and time again and now is the time  to take action  but obviously we were running an  interview with one of the republican  leaders who was very cross when our  reporter tried to put it to him that it  was an american problem and it was to do  with gun laws he said it's to do with  mental health problems  the gun lobby in america is is  incredibly popular and incredibly  powerful can you can you kind of shed  any light on that you know you obviously  looked into it a lot why is it so  powerful in america  money  it's the only thing that matters to  these people these politicians the  republicans you look at their bank  accounts you look at how they're funded  the nra funds these republican  politicians and that's where they get  all of their support and  all it takes is for every single  campaign every single politician that is  campaigning against gun control measures  look at their nra rating look how much  money they've been given by the nra to  support their campaigns and you'll see  that's exactly why they're against any  gun control legislation  so how do you think  things could change in america every  time there is a shooting like this you  know after sandy hook everybody always  says oh this is the event this is the  thing that changes things remember obama  standing up and making this really  powerful speech and biden this week  again making a really powerful speech we  even had sports leaders breaking down in  tears at press conferences  and yet nothing changes does it what  will make them change  it's got to be across the board there's  got to be a joint effort for both  republicans and democrats and it's got  to be incremental there's not going to  be one piece of legislation that fixes  this it's got to be from the bottom up  and from the top downs they've got  grassroots organizations that are  campaigning and they're doing the best  work that they can but we need to change  from the top down and the bills are  there there's a background check that's  been sitting with the senate for over a  year now and it needs to be passed and  that's that's where they've got to start  they've got to start by doing just  something that first step on the journey  and as soon as they can do that it will  start to change it's going to take so  much time but they need to make the  first steps  do you have any confidence it will  happen like for example in your lifetime  when you look at the current makeup of  the senate  you've got to have some hope  and  the hope comes from watching the  campaigners in america just now people  like manny and patricia oliver had  changed the wretch the kids after  parkland they're the most powerful  campaigners i've ever seen and these are  the people that are going to make the  changes that america needs  and when we think about about your  sister emma just tell us a little bit  about her and and how old she was when  you lost her  i never i don't have any memories of  emma she was five when she was killed  and done playing i was only two years  old  and  it's difficult having that not having  any memories of someone that you should  have there you know i'm so close with my  sister ellie and she's my best friend in  the world and not having that yeah um as  my big sister that i should have  and that's what makes me  support all of these people in america  there's so many families there's so many  brothers and sisters and parents that  have lost kids that  so senselessly lost they don't need to  have died and there's things that we can  do there's things there's changes that  can't happen that can prevent this and i  think that's the reason that i speak and  the only reason i ever you know give my  voice to these people is because  you know we've been through it and  we people don't need to go through this  anymore  jack thank you for sharing your voice  with us today jack crozier whose sister  emma died in the dunblane shooting thank  you very much  thank you
 

